From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Arun Iyengar <aiyengar1990@gmail.com>
Date: Tue, 10 Nov 2026 00:33:06 +0000
Subject: media: cedrus: report the AVS2 AFBC picture offset in the
 compose rectangle

The AVS2 decoder writes its AFBC output 8 lines down, where the H.265
decoder on the same engine writes it 4 lines down. Shown from row 0, a
10-bit AVS2 AFBC buffer is 8 lines too low.

Found with the DE33 writeback: AFBC captures of a 1080p 10-bit AVS2
stream (SAO and ALF on) match the P010 captures moved 8 rows down and 0
columns across (mean luma difference 0.11, against 12 for the next best
shift), while the P010 output matches the reference decoder.
The vendor library fills in the same AFBC header top_crop of 4 for AVS2
as for H.265, so it cannot be taken from there.

Report top 8 through the CAPTURE compose selection for AVS2 AFBC, as is
done for H.265. Size the AFBC surfaces for a picture coded in whole 8x8
blocks plus the 8 lines above it; for heights that are a multiple of 8
the sizes do not change.

Assisted-by: LLM
Signed-off-by: Arun Iyengar <aiyengar1990@gmail.com>
---
 drivers/staging/media/sunxi/cedrus/cedrus.h   |  9 ++++++--
 .../staging/media/sunxi/cedrus/cedrus_video.c | 22 +++++++++++++------
 2 files changed, 22 insertions(+), 9 deletions(-)

diff --git a/drivers/staging/media/sunxi/cedrus/cedrus.h b/drivers/staging/media/sunxi/cedrus/cedrus.h
index afb3a4b..886cc9f 100644
--- a/drivers/staging/media/sunxi/cedrus/cedrus.h
+++ b/drivers/staging/media/sunxi/cedrus/cedrus.h
@@ -553,10 +553,15 @@ static inline bool cedrus_fmt_is_scaled(struct cedrus_ctx *ctx,
 	       fmt->width < ctx->src_fmt.width;
 }
 
-/* Size of a 10-bit YUV 4:2:0 AFBC 16x16 split surface. */
+/*
+ * Size of a 10-bit YUV 4:2:0 AFBC 16x16 split surface. The decoders write
+ * the picture, coded in whole 8x8 blocks, up to 8 lines down (4 for H.265,
+ * 8 for AVS2, see cedrus_dst_picture_top()), so the surface covers the
+ * aligned height plus 8 lines.
+ */
 static inline u32 cedrus_afbc_10_size(unsigned int width, unsigned int height)
 {
-	return DIV_ROUND_UP(width, 16) * DIV_ROUND_UP(height + 4, 16) *
+	return DIV_ROUND_UP(width, 16) * DIV_ROUND_UP(ALIGN(height, 8) + 8, 16) *
 	       (512 + 16) + 32 + SZ_1K;
 }
 
diff --git a/drivers/staging/media/sunxi/cedrus/cedrus_video.c b/drivers/staging/media/sunxi/cedrus/cedrus_video.c
index 54232d6..f2ee563 100644
--- a/drivers/staging/media/sunxi/cedrus/cedrus_video.c
+++ b/drivers/staging/media/sunxi/cedrus/cedrus_video.c
@@ -827,21 +827,29 @@ static int cedrus_s_fmt_vid_out(struct file *file, void *priv,
  * picture row 0 and the surface is 4 lines taller than the picture (hence
  * the "height + 4" in the AFBC buffer sizes). The vendor library describes
  * this to its display driver as an AFBC header top_crop of 4 for H.265
- * (HevcInitAfbcInfo()). VP9 AFBC output starts at row 0.
+ * (HevcInitAfbcInfo()). The AVS2 decoder, which runs on the same engine,
+ * writes its AFBC output 8 lines down, although the vendor library fills
+ * in the same top_crop of 4 for it. VP9 AFBC output starts at row 0.
  */
 static unsigned int cedrus_dst_picture_top(struct cedrus_ctx *ctx)
 {
-	if (cedrus_dst_is_afbc(ctx) &&
-	    ctx->src_fmt.pixelformat == V4L2_PIX_FMT_HEVC_SLICE)
-		return 4;
+	if (!cedrus_dst_is_afbc(ctx))
+		return 0;
 
-	return 0;
+	switch (ctx->src_fmt.pixelformat) {
+	case V4L2_PIX_FMT_HEVC_SLICE:
+		return 4;
+	case V4L2_PIX_FMT_AVS2_FRAME:
+		return 8;
+	default:
+		return 0;
+	}
 }
 
 /*
  * The compose rectangle tells userspace where the decoded picture lies in
- * a CAPTURE buffer, so that a consumer of an H.265 AFBC buffer (e.g. a DRM
- * plane) can skip the rows above it. It is fixed by the format.
+ * a CAPTURE buffer, so that a consumer of an H.265 or AVS2 AFBC buffer
+ * (e.g. a DRM plane) can skip the rows above it. It is fixed by the format.
  */
 static int cedrus_g_selection(struct file *file, void *priv,
 			      struct v4l2_selection *s)
